{
  "note": "Sensory Modalities",
  "source": "bf9cc13cc4bb",
  "script": "1ed9aba686b7",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "2e7f7f773d6a",
  "duration": 88.66,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Sensory Modalities",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "A sensory modality is each unique type of sensation.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 3.52
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, it is every distinct quality of experience, such as touch, pain or vision, that the nervous system detects through its own class of receptor.",
          "start": 3.84,
          "end": 13.2
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll begin with the somatic modalities, and then move on to the special senses and the visceral ones.",
          "start": 13.44,
          "end": 18.64
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Somatic modalities",
      "start": 19.487,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The somatic modalities gather information from the skin and from the deep tissues.",
          "start": 19.487,
          "end": 24.407
        },
        {
          "text": "Touch, pressure and vibration are mechanical stimuli, and they are detected by mechanoreceptors.",
          "start": 24.447,
          "end": 30.527
        },
        {
          "text": "These receptors are distributed through the epidermis, the dermis and the deep tissue, so mechanical energy is sampled at different depths.",
          "start": 30.607,
          "end": 38.567
        },
        {
          "text": "Alongside touch, pressure and vibration sits proprioception, which is the sense of the position and movement of the body.",
          "start": 38.607,
          "end": 45.647
        },
        {
          "text": "Then there is temperature, detected by thermoreceptors, and finally pain, detected by nociceptors.",
          "start": 45.727,
          "end": 52.447
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Special senses and visceral modalities",
      "start": 52.95,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now, the same receptor logic extends to the special senses and to the viscera.",
          "start": 52.95,
          "end": 57.91
        },
        {
          "text": "Mechanical stimuli are also detected by hair cells, which mediate hearing and balance.",
          "start": 58.03,
          "end": 63.47
        },
        {
          "text": "Baroreceptors detect mechanical stimuli as well, and they monitor blood pressure.",
          "start": 63.51,
          "end": 68.497
        },
        {
          "text": "Electromagnetic radiation, meanwhile, is detected by the rods and cones.",
          "start": 68.603,
          "end": 73.15
        },
        {
          "text": "Last of all, chemoreceptors cover taste and smell.",
          "start": 73.19,
          "end": 76.67
        },
        {
          "text": "They also cover internal chemical variables, such as glucose and the partial pressures of oxygen and carbon dioxide.",
          "start": 76.71,
          "end": 83.55
        },
        {
          "text": "That rounds off the sensory modalities, each one matched to its own class of receptor.",
          "start": 83.59,
          "end": 88.71
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "segments": [
